# =============================================================================
# FALCONAge
# =============================================================================
# The working tree holds a good deal that is not the package: upstream clones
# read for reference, working notes, a 586 MB test corpus fetched from GEO and
# Hugging Face. All of it is reproducible from a URL and none of it belongs in
# the history. Everything else is the ordinary Python/R/Quarto/Docker set.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- the repository root is allow-list only ----------------------------------
# `/*` matches only entries directly in the root, so the per-directory rules
# further down still apply normally. Everything at the top level is excluded and
# the deliverables are re-admitted by name below.
#
# Written this way round on purpose. A deny-list of working material has to be
# extended by hand every time something new is put in the root, and it fails
# silently and permanently the one time somebody forgets: the file is simply
# committed. An allow-list fails the other way, by leaving something untracked
# until it is noticed, which `git status` will show you.
#
# Re-admitting a directory only means git may descend into it; the rules below
# still decide what inside it is tracked.
/*
!/.dockerignore
!/.gitattributes
!/.github/
!/.gitignore
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/CITATION.cff
!/LICENSE
!/NEWS.md
!/PUBLISHING.md
!/README.md
!/ROADMAP.md
!/colorscheme.yaml
!/docker/
!/docs/
!/logo/
!/python/
!/r/
!/test/

# ---- test data --------------------------------------------------------------
# Deny everything under test/data/, then re-admit the four files that ARE the
# deliverable: the manifest and the two fetchers that read it, plus the notes.
#
# Written deny-first on purpose. The obvious alternative -- listing the group
# directories the fetcher creates -- silently stops protecting anything the day
# someone adds a group to datasets.yaml, and the failure mode is a 90 MB parquet
# in a commit. A deny-list that has to be widened by hand cannot fail that way.
#
# test/data itself is NOT excluded (the pattern needs at least one component
# after data/), so git still descends into it and the negations below take
# effect. Group directories match, are excluded, and are never descended into.
/test/data/**
!/test/data/README.md
!/test/data/datasets.yaml
!/test/data/fetch_test_data.py
!/test/data/fetch_test_data.R
!/test/data/.gitignore
!/test/README.md
!/test/run_all.py
!/test/gpu_check.py

# ---- test outputs ------------------------------------------------------------
# Regenerated by test/run_all.py on every run and overwritten in place. Scores,
# manifests, figures and a self-contained HTML report per input dataset -- all
# reproducible from the corpus plus the code, none of it worth a diff.
# test/README.md is the interpretation of these outputs and IS tracked; the
# tables inside it are rewritten by the same script, so the documentation
# cannot drift from what the code produces.
/test/output/**
!/test/output/.gitkeep

# ---- figures ARE tracked, unlike the rest of the output tree -----------------
# Deliberately the exception. The figures are what the README and both sets of
# documentation point at, and a reader who clones the repository should see them
# without first downloading 586 MB of corpus and running the pipeline. They are
# small (PNG, a few hundred kB each), they change only when the code or the
# colour scheme changes, and a diff that says "this figure moved" is exactly the
# review signal you want when someone edits colorscheme.yaml.
# Only the gallery: one figure per TYPE, about 20 PNGs and two megabytes. The
# full per-dataset tree beside it is five hundred near-duplicate figures and
# forty megabytes -- the right thing to have on disk after a run, the wrong
# thing to put in a repository.
/test/output_figures/**
!/test/output_figures/gallery/
!/test/output_figures/gallery/**

# ---- Python ------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
__pycache__
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
build/
develop-eggs/
dist/
eggs/
.eggs/
sdist/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
*.egg
.installed.cfg
MANIFEST

# Environments. uv.lock is deliberately NOT ignored -- the Docker images install
# from it, so an unpinned image and a pinned one would disagree in the seventh
# decimal of a PC rotation and the gold-standard tests would start flapping.
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
.python-version

# Test and type-checker state
.pytest_cache/
.hypothesis/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.nox/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
*.cover

# ---- R -----------------------------------------------------------------------
.Rproj.user/
.Rhistory
.RData
.Ruserdata
*.Rproj
*.Rcheck/
*.tar.gz
r/src/*.o
r/src/*.so
r/src/*.dll
r/..Rcheck/
r/inst/doc/
# man/ is roxygen output and NEWS.md is generated from CHANGELOG.md, but both
# ship in the CRAN tarball and CRAN requires them present, so both stay tracked.

# ---- documentation -----------------------------------------------------------
# Quarto and pkgdown both render into the tree. The rendered site is published
# by CI from a clean checkout; a committed copy only ever goes stale.
docs/_site/
docs/.quarto/
docs/reference/          # quartodoc output, generated from docstrings
docs/r/                  # pkgdown output, generated from roxygen
.quarto/
r/docs/

# ---- weights and caches ------------------------------------------------------
# Coefficient files land in the HF cache, not the tree. A tier C clock's
# user-supplied file must never be committed -- that is the redistribution the
# licence forbids, and the whole point of README section 6.
.cache/
hf_cache/
*.safetensors
*.pt
*.pth
licensed_weights/

# ---- run outputs -------------------------------------------------------------
results/
results_*/
*.h5ad
!r/inst/extdata/*.h5ad
!python/tests/**/*.h5ad
run_manifest.json
report.html

# ---- editors and OS ----------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ---- scratch -----------------------------------------------------------------
tmp/
*.log
!docker/*.log

ROADMAP.md